    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nFourth-Gen Peugeot 308 Gets Radical Virtual Fastback Makeover Inspired by Paris Motor Show Concepts\n9 photos\nPhoto: avarvarii / Instagram\nStellantis wanted to show that it cares about classic automotive shows, and since the 2026 Mondial de l’Auto is considered as one of its home turf events, it arrived in full force with over 60 new vehicles and no less than nine concepts among them.\nThey even organized a ‘The Night Before’ event, just like during the good old days before Covid, where they presented six of the prototypes ahead of the official reveal in front of the general audience. Most of the important brands got their five minutes of fame in front of the 2026 Paris Motor Show spotlights at the Paris Expo Porte de Versailles venue, and of course the French brand Peugeot was a chief among them.\nMore precisely, the near-premium French marque showcased two concepts, the RCZ Vision and the E-T16 , with the first one bringing back the RCZ double-bubble-roofed coupe for a new stint and the other paying homage to the Peugeot 205 T16 in a retro-futuristic hot hatch manner with ludicrous EV power. Both of them are brand manifestos, revealing the new design direction the French brand is about to take.\nSure, they might also become real, reviving the RCZ and paving the way for an EV to rival the bonkers Renault 5 Turbo 3E hot hatchback. But it’s more likely that most of their styling elements will trickle down to regular models – including the upcoming fourth generation of the Peugeot 308 compact car, at least according to the rumor mill.\nMore precisely, a member of the imaginative guild of digital car content creators dwelling across the parallel universes of vehicular CGI has worked for the rumor mill to uncover the new generation’s digital secrets in their latest unofficial, hypothetical design project. Andrei Avarvarii, the virtual artist behind Avarvarii Automotive Artworks (also known as ' avarvarii ' on social media), has taken a thorough, in-depth CGI look at Peugeot's upcoming plans for the good folks over at Auto Express and came up with this virtual proposition.\nBecause we have already seen that some of the latest Peugeot models like the 408 or even the latest 3008 have slightly wackier designs than usual, “these illustrations envision a bold new direction for the next-generation Peugeot 308.” According to the author and the publication, “rather than retaining its traditional two-box hatchback proportions, the design transitions into a sleek, aerodynamic fastback.”\nInspiration comes from the Peugeot Inception and the two Paris Motor Show concepts, but also from the larger 408, so “the front fascia features a sharp, angular profile, a seamless body-colored grille, and a razor-thin evolution of the brand's signature three-claw LED lighting.” Sculpted geometric elements adorn the sides, along with aero-optimized wheels and a raked roofline, while the back has a “muscular, high-set tail.”\nThe new triple-layer full-width LED taillights are also present, in an interpretation set between the serious E-T16 and the exotic RCZ Vision concept. As for powertrains, the news outlet muses that the new STLA One platform will make it EV -first but also capable of gasoline and hybrid setups if needed. The new model will probably feature the new cockpit architecture inside as well as the pioneering ‘cell-to-body’ setup where the battery is part of the floor structure like in BYD or Tesla models, among others. View this post on Instagram A post shared by Avarvarii Automotive Artworks (@avarvarii)\nVia // Auto Express\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPeugeot 308 Peugeot 308 fastback auto express CGI new generation rendering avarvarii\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT VW’s Tiguan R Spied, It’s a High-Riding Golf R",
